  {
   "n": 1,
   "qid": "1",
   "part": "A",
   "topic": "General Aptitude",
   "subtopic": "Direction sense",
   "type": "single",
   "body": "If a map is placed in such a manner that southwest becomes east, then what will north become?",
   "options": [
    "Northeast",
    "Southwest",
    "Northwest",
    "Southeast"
   ],
   "answer": [
    2
   ],
   "given": "The map is rotated rigidly, and under that rotation the southwest direction ends up pointing east.",
   "concept": "Rotating a map turns every direction by the same angle. Put the eight compass points on a circle at $45^\\circ$ spacing, find the single angle that carries SW to E, then apply it to N.",
   "steps": [
    "<b>Put numbers on the compass.</b> Measuring clockwise from north: N $=0^\\circ$, NE $=45^\\circ$, E $=90^\\circ$, SE $=135^\\circ$, S $=180^\\circ$, SW $=225^\\circ$, W $=270^\\circ$, NW $=315^\\circ$.",
    "<b>Find the rotation.</b> SW sits at $225^\\circ$ and must land on E at $90^\\circ$, so the rotation $\\theta$ satisfies $225^\\circ+\\theta\\equiv 90^\\circ \\pmod{360^\\circ}$, giving $\\theta = -135^\\circ$, that is $225^\\circ$ clockwise.",
    "<b>Apply it to north.</b> N is at $0^\\circ$, so it moves to $0^\\circ+225^\\circ = 225^\\circ$.",
    "<b>Read the answer off the compass.</b> $225^\\circ$ is southwest."
   ],
   "conclusion": "North becomes <b>southwest</b>, option (2).",
   "pitfall": "Rotating by the wrong amount because SW and E look like a $180^\\circ$ flip. They are $135^\\circ$ apart, not $180^\\circ$, so the two directions are not opposite and the naive answer northeast is wrong."
  },
  {
   "n": 2,
   "qid": "2",
   "part": "A",
   "topic": "General Aptitude",
   "subtopic": "Logarithmic scales",
   "type": "single",
   "body": "If water of pH 8 is diluted 100 times with neutral water (pH $=7$) then it will",
   "options": [
    "become acidic.",
    "remain basic.",
    "become neutral.",
    "become heavy."
   ],
   "answer": [
    2
   ],
   "given": "A solution at pH $8$, so mildly basic, diluted a hundredfold with water that is exactly neutral.",
   "concept": "Dilution moves a solution towards neutrality but can never carry it past neutrality. Adding neutral water removes no excess hydroxide, it only spreads it through more volume, so the excess shrinks towards zero and pH approaches $7$ from above without reaching it.",
   "steps": [
    "<b>Read the starting concentrations.</b> pH $8$ means $[\\mathrm{H^+}]=10^{-8}$ and, from $[\\mathrm{H^+}][\\mathrm{OH^-}]=10^{-14}$, $[\\mathrm{OH^-}]=10^{-6}$ mol/L.",
    "<b>Identify what actually gets diluted.</b> What makes the solution basic is the <em>excess</em> of hydroxide over the neutral value, namely $10^{-6}-10^{-7}=9\\times10^{-7}$ mol/L. Diluting a hundredfold leaves an excess of $9\\times10^{-9}$ mol/L.",
    "<b>Solve for the new pH.</b> With $h=[\\mathrm{H^+}]$, charge balance gives $\\dfrac{10^{-14}}{h}-h = 9\\times10^{-9}$, that is $h^{2}+9\\times10^{-9}h-10^{-14}=0$, whose positive root is $h\\approx 9.56\\times10^{-8}$.",
    "<b>Convert back.</b> $\\mathrm{pH} = -\\log_{10}(9.56\\times10^{-8}) \\approx 7.02$, which is above $7$.",
    "<b>Interpret.</b> The solution has moved very close to neutral but is still on the basic side, and no amount of neutral water will push it below $7$."
   ],
   "conclusion": "It <b>remains basic</b>, option (2).",
   "pitfall": "Subtracting $\\log_{10}100 = 2$ from the pH to get $6$ and calling the result acidic. pH is not additive under dilution like that; treating it so would let you turn a base into an acid with pure water."
  },
  {
   "n": 3,
   "qid": "3",
   "part": "A",
   "topic": "General Aptitude",
   "subtopic": "Combinations",
   "type": "single",
   "body": "Fifteen distinct points are randomly placed on the circumference of a circle. How many distinct straight lines at the most can be formed by pairs among these points?",
   "options": [
    "105",
    "455",
    "30",
    "210"
   ],
   "answer": [
    1
   ],
   "given": "Fifteen distinct points, all lying on one circle.",
   "concept": "Two points determine one line, so the count is a combination. The circle matters because no three points of a circle are ever collinear, so no two different pairs give the same line.",
   "steps": [
    "<b>Count the pairs.</b> A line needs two points, and order does not matter, so the number of pairs is $$\\binom{15}{2}=\\frac{15\\times14}{2}=105.$$",
    "<b>Check that the lines are distinct.</b> A straight line meets a circle in at most two points, so no three of the fifteen points are collinear. Different pairs therefore give different lines and there is no over-counting.",
    "<b>Conclude.</b> All $105$ lines are distinct, so $105$ is both the maximum and the exact count."
   ],
   "conclusion": "There are <b>105</b> distinct lines, option (1).",
   "pitfall": "Using $\\binom{15}{3}=455$, which counts triangles rather than lines, or $15\\times 14 = 210$, which counts ordered pairs and so counts every line twice."
  },
  {
   "n": 4,
   "qid": "4",
   "part": "A",
   "topic": "General Aptitude",
   "subtopic": "Exponential growth",
   "type": "single",
   "body": "In a population of microbial cells, the initial population is $50$, and the growth rate is $0.1$ per hour. If the population grows exponentially, what will be the approximate size of the population after $10$ hours?",
   "options": [
    "51",
    "82",
    "136",
    "156"
   ],
   "answer": [
    3
   ],
   "given": "$N_0 = 50$ cells, continuous growth rate $r = 0.1$ per hour, elapsed time $t = 10$ hours.",
   "concept": "Exponential growth at a continuous rate $r$ obeys $N(t) = N_0 e^{rt}$. The product $rt$ here is exactly $1$, which makes the arithmetic a single multiplication by $e$.",
   "steps": [
    "<b>Write the model.</b> $N(t) = N_0 e^{rt}$.",
    "<b>Substitute.</b> $N(10) = 50\\,e^{0.1\\times 10} = 50\\,e^{1}$.",
    "<b>Evaluate.</b> $e \\approx 2.71828$, so $N(10) \\approx 50\\times 2.71828 = 135.9$.",
    "<b>Round.</b> Cells are whole, so the population is about $136$."
   ],
   "conclusion": "The population is approximately <b>136</b>, option (3).",
   "pitfall": "Using simple growth, $50(1+0.1\\times 10) = 100$, or compounding discretely as $50(1.1)^{10}\\approx 130$. The phrase &ldquo;grows exponentially&rdquo; with a rate <em>per hour</em> means the continuous model $e^{rt}$."
  },
